  moduli ruled out by the MSB bound (<= 13 states): 2..13  (12 moduli)
  moduli ruled out by the LSB bound (<= 11 states): [2, 3, 4]  (3 moduli)
  moduli ruled out by the direct sweep: 2..20,000 (19,999 moduli)

  Read it the right way round:
   * on congruences the direct sweep beats the SAT bounds by 1,538x;
   * on everything else the SAT bounds are the only statement there is,
     since a union of residue classes is a vanishing fraction of the
     2-automatic sets of any given size;
   * the LSB bound, the headline for most of this program, is the
     weakest of the three on this axis: 3 moduli.
  The MSB/LSB gap here is the SAME asymmetry the solver timings
  measured -- m states against m*ord_2(m) -- showing up as a
  statement about content rather than about seconds.
